  "verbalization": "Isradipine, a calcium channel antagonist, is linked to Cacna1c through a series of molecular interactions. The evidence suggests that Isradipine, as part of the class of dihydropyridines, binds to Nisoldipine, which in turn interacts with Cacna1c. This relationship is supported by the shared ancestry of Isradipine and Nisoldipine, with both falling under the broader category of methyl esters and calcium channel blockers. The lowest common ancestor of these entities, Methyl Ester, highlights a common structural feature that may facilitate the binding process. The integration of these pathways underscores a mechanism where Isradipine, through its class and molecular interactions, influences Cacna1c, likely by modulating calcium channel activity. The evidence points to a direct, albeit indirect, relationship through the shared pharmacological profile and molecular interactions, reinforcing the role of Isradipine in targeting Cacna1c.",
